/** @page python_qore_importguide Importing Qore APIs into Python

    @tableofcontents

    %Qore APIs and definitions can be imported into a %Python program by using the following module command:
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(python) import-ns <qore-namespace> <python-module-path>@endcode

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
Program p(PO_NEW_STYLE);
p.issueModuleCmd("python", "import-ns MyQoreNamespace MyPythonModule");
    @endcode

    @section python_qore_objects Qore Objects in Python

    A %Qore object is the same %Python object each time it is passed to %Python code in an interpreter, so \c is,
    \c == and \c hash() work as for %Python objects.  The public members of a %Qore object are attributes of its
    %Python object:
    - reading a member that the object does not have raises \c AttributeError (also for private members), so
      \c hasattr() and \c getattr() with a default follow %Python semantics; a member whose value was removed is
      missing as with %Qore's \c exists operator; special names (ex: \c __fspath__) are never %Qore members; for a
      class with a \c memberGate() method, the method gives the values of the members that the object does not have
    - setting an attribute sets the %Qore member (\c del removes it); setting a private member or a member that a
      class with declared members does not declare raises \c AttributeError; on the object of a %Python subclass,
      attributes that are not %Qore members (and properties of the %Python class) are %Python attributes
    - \c dir() lists the public members of the %Qore object as well
    - %Python can create weak references to %Qore objects (\c weakref)

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
import qoreloader
from qore.__root__.Qore.Thread import Queue, Mutex

q = Queue()
m = Mutex()
q.push(m)
assert q.get() is m
assert getattr(q, 'nope', None) is None
    @endcode

    @section python_qore_declarations Static Members, Enums and Hashdecls

    - the public static members of a %Qore class are attributes of its %Python class (and objects) that give and set
      the current value of the member (\c qoreloader.QoreStaticMember descriptors); setting the attribute of an
      object sets the member, while assigning to the attribute of the class replaces the descriptor, as for any
      %Python class attribute
    - a %Qore enum is a %Python enum class: an \c enum.IntEnum if its values are integers, an \c enum.StrEnum if they
      are strings, otherwise an \c enum.Enum; a value of the %Qore enum is the member of the %Python enum class, and
      the member is the value of the %Qore enum in %Qore (an integer or a string is not, as in %Qore)
    - a hashdecl is a \c dict subclass whose constructor sets the default values of the hashdecl's keys and then its
      arguments (as \c dict.update()); a hash of the hashdecl is an object of the class, and an object of the class
      is a hash of the hashdecl in %Qore (a \c dict is not, as a hash is not in %Qore); unknown keys and values of
      the wrong types raise a %Qore exception when the object is passed to %Qore

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
import qoreloader
from qore.__root__.Qore import StatInfo, hstat

info = hstat('/tmp')
# an object of the StatInfo dict subclass
print(type(info).__name__, info['mode'])
    @endcode

    @section python_inheriting_qore_classes Inheriting Qore Classes in Python

    When %Python code inherits %Qore classes, the %Qore constructor must be explicitly called in the %Python
    \c __init__() method as in the following example.

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
import qore.xml

class PyXmlRpcClient(qore.xml.XmlRpcClient):
    def __init__(self, opts, do_not_connect):
        super(PyXmlRpcClient, self).__init__(opts, do_not_connect)
    @endcode

    @section python_aliases Creating Python Aliases

    The following module command can be used to create an alias in %Python code:
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(python) alias <python-source-path> <python-target-path>@endcode

    This is often used when importing %Qore namespaces to %Python to create an alias directly from a child %Qore
    namespace to a %Python module or package that is more convenient to access in %Python code.

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
Program p(PO_NEW_STYLE);
p.issueModuleCmd("python", "import-ns MyQoreNamespace MyPythonModule");
p.issueModuleCmd("python", "alias MyPythonModule.MySubModule1.MySubModule2.MyApi MyApi");
    @endcode
*/
